# Move, zoom, rotate, and flip

Image transforms change the picture under the selection. Moving or resizing the stencil changes the selection itself. Both affect the resulting crop, but serve different controls.

## Pan and zoom

Bind a `CropperInstance` and call `moveImage(left, top)` or `zoomImage(factor)`. Zoom factors are relative: `2` zooms in, `0.5` zooms out. Core restrictions limit the result.

<Example source={TransformImageExampleSource} title="TransformImageExample" files={{ 'SquareButton.svelte': TransformImageExampleFile0, 'VerticalButtons.svelte': TransformImageExampleFile1 }}>
	<TransformImageExample />
</Example>

For a slider that describes an absolute zoom level, follow [Absolute zoom](/docs/tutorials/absolute-zoom). For mouse, touch and wheel configuration, see [Gestures](/docs/guides/gestures).

## Rotate and flip

`rotateImage(angle)` adds a rotation in degrees. `flipImage(horizontal, vertical)` flips the selected axes. Quarter turns are the straightforward choice with the default restrictions; arbitrary-angle workflows may need an appropriate custom strategy, as in the mobile [showcase](/docs/showcase).

<Example source={RotateImageExampleSource} title="RotateImageExample" files={{ 'SquareButton.svelte': RotateImageExampleFile0, 'VerticalButtons.svelte': RotateImageExampleFile1 }}>
	<RotateImageExample />
</Example>

Transforms are part of cropper state and are included in canvas export. Coordinates after rotation belong to the transformed image space. Sending only an unrotated rectangle to a server will not reproduce that result; include transforms or export the canvas.

<Example source={RotateCoordinatesExampleSource} title="RotateCoordinatesExample">
	<RotateCoordinatesExample />
</Example>

## Transitions and completion

Action options can disable animation with `{ transitions: false }`. A continuously dragged control should use that option so it follows the pointer. Gesture primitives pair transformation calls with `transformImageEnd()`; custom stencil movement pairs with `moveCoordinatesEnd()` or `resizeCoordinatesEnd()`.

To set the opening rotation and flips, use [default transforms](/docs/guides/defaults-and-restrictions#default-transforms). Changing those defaults does not replace an explicit action on the current image.
